# Coupled assimilations are a collection of component assimilations,
# each in a traditional CESM2 "CASE" directory, but all are controlled by
# a CASE called "CESM", which runs the hindcast and manages the assimilations. 
# The component directories will be named according to the chosen comp_names 
# from DART/models/{comp_name}, including "CESM",
# The hindcast CASE (CESM), and then assimilating CASEs ("assimilators"; cam-fv,...), 
# will be built in an EXPeriment directory, defined by the user 
# as part of the CESM2 CASE creation process.  This name (EXP) should be unique
# (in contrast to the CASE names, which are restricted).
# After setting up the cases, the CASEs directory structure should look like
# CASEROOTs:
#   .../your_cases/EXP/         (experiment directory, not a CASE)
#       CESM/                   (hindcast and assimilation organizer CASE)
#         case.submit           (Only this case is submitted) 
#         multi_assim.csh       (calls the component assimilation.cshs)
#         DART_config
#         DART_params
#       cam-fv/                 (an assimilator CASE)
#         assimilation.csh
#         DART_config
#       clm/                    (another assimilator CASE)
#         assimilation.csh
#         DART_config
#       ...
#   
#   CIME_OUTPUT_ROOTs:
#   .../scratch/USER/EXP/
#       CESM/
#         run
#         bld
#       cam-fv/
#         run
#         bld
#       ...

# This resource file has configuration items needed 
# when building and running CESM2 coupled assimilations with DART.
# This file will be copied into the EXP CASEROOT directories to be used 
# by the CESM CASE and the assimilators.
# This does not contain all of the parameters you may want to set.
# See the setup script, input.nml, and DART documentation.
    
# The workflow of setting up a coupled assimilation is:
# 1) Edit DART_params (this file) to define the EXP and assimilationS.
# 2) Edit and run the CESM component setup script (./setup_assimilation) 
#    to create the CASE directory structure which is used in the next step.
# 3) Edit and run the setup scripts (including DART_config) for each CESM component 
#    which will assimilate observations (an "assimilator").
#    Each assimilator CASE should be built parallel to the CESM CASE defined in 2).
# 4) Edit and run the CESM DART_config, which will check for the correct setup
#    of the assimilators.
# 5) Run a cycle of this CESM case.
#
# All of CESM stops every 6 hours so that an ensemble of state files is available
# for each assimilator at the frequency of typical atmospheric assimilations.
# In the future each assimilator may have # its own frequency 
# (e.g. ocean assimilated daily), but there are questions to be resolved
# before that can happen:
#   Which cross-assimilations should be run at those frequencies?
#   Questions about inflation, etc..

# ==============================================================================
# Big picture parameters.
#
# EXP           A name for the whole assimilation (not a CASENAME).
#               It will be used many ways; directory and file names
#               both locally and (possibly) on the long-term archive, 
#               and script names, so consider its length and information content.
# cesmtag       The version of the CESM source code to use when building the code.
#               The multi_assim.csh in this directory will handle only cesm2_1 and later.
# compset       Defines the CESM model components, vertical resolution and physics packages to be used.
#               For now must be a CAM-FV "F" compset, either supported, or use the
#               --run-unsupported option.  Later B and other compsets may be accommodated.
#               A compset defined specifically for CAM assimilations is
#               FHIST_DARTC6 = HIST_CAM60_CLM50%SP_CICE%PRES_DOCN%DOM_SROF_SGLC_SWAV
#               For a list of the pre-defined component sets: $cesmtag/cime/scripts/query_config --compsets
#               To create a variant compset, see the CESM documentation
#               https://ncar.github.io/CAM/doc/build/html/users_guide/atmospheric-configurations.html
#               and carefully incorporate any needed changes into the setup script.
# resolution    Defines the horizontal resolution and dynamics; see CESM docs.
#               see http://esmci.github.io/cime/users_guide/grids.html.
#               f19_f19  ... FV core at ~ 2 degree (19 means 1.9 degrees of latitude).
#               f09_f09  ... FV core at ~ 1 degree (the 2nd f09 means CLM uses a .9 degree latitude grid)
#               To use the high resolution SST data ocean, use resolution "f09_d025" or "f19_d025"
#               and the user_grid variable.
#               This script handles CESM2 only.  CESM3 is not friendly to CAM-FV,
#               and there is not yet (2026-9) an ne##_d025 grid for CAM-SE.
# num_instances The number of ensemble members.

# assimilators  Define the coupled assimilation by listing the assimilating components
# cross_assims  and the cross-assimilations.  The self-assimilations will always be done
#               because that's how the obs estimates and increments are generated
#               for use by all assimilating components.
#               Each assimilator will assimilate obs from other components as separate phases.
#               The assimilation name format is "assimilator<obs_component",
#               where both pieces are the names of models in DART: cam-fv, clm, ...
#               (This allows the scripts to find files using parts of the assimilation names.)
#               The list of cross_assims enables the script to do full strongly-coupled, weakly-coupled,
#               and intermediate (e.g.;'clm<cam-fv' but not 'cam-fv<clm'), as the user selects.
# comp_file_bits  The part of CESM file names which denotes the component.
#                 These are hard-coded in the components' source code, so we can't query them.
#                 The choices (cesm2_2) are: cam, clm2,  pop, mom,  rtm, mosart,  cice, cice5, (cice6?)

# user_grid     Provides arguments to create_newcase enabling the use of hi-res SST data files.
# compset_args  Like user_grid, but for non-standard compsets.

# ==============================================================================

setenv EXP            S-C_test0
setenv cesmtag        my_cesm_sandbox
setenv compset         HIST_CAM60_CLM50%BGC-CROP_CICE%PRES_DOCN%DOM_MOSART_SGLC_SWAV_SIAC
# CLM setenv comp        2000_DATM%GSWP3v1_CLM50%BGC-CROP_SICE_SOCN_MOSART_SGLC_SWAV
setenv resolution     f09_f09_mg17
setenv num_instances  5

setenv assimilators ('cam-fv' 'clm')
setenv cross_assims ('cam-fv<clm' 'clm<cam-fv')
setenv shared_input_nml 'input.nml.cam-fv+clm'
setenv comp_file_bits ('cam' 'clm2')

setenv user_grid      ''
setenv compset_args "--compset $compset"
# If cemstag >= cesm2_0_alpha10f and compset = FHIST_DARTC6 (or another non-supported):
# and resolution is non-standard (e.g. d025)
setenv compset_args "${compset_args} --run-unsupported"


# ==============================================================================
# Directories:
# dartroot      Location of the root of _your_ DART installation.
# dartscripts   The directories in models/{CESM models}/shell_scripts which have 
#               the scripts required by this assimilation.
#               WARNING; this assumes that the scripts directory for each model
#                        has the same name.
# COMP_obspath  Location of obs_seq.out files for component COMP
#               User must provide one for each of the components listed as $assimilators (above).
# obspaths      Locations of the obs used by $assimilators.  Must match 1-to-1.
setenv dartroot       /glade/work/${USER}/DART/Manhattan_git
setenv dartscripts    cesm2_2_cpld
setenv atm_obspath    '/glade/p/cisl/dares/Observations/NCEP+ACARS+GPS' 
setenv lnd_obspath    '/glade/campaign/cisl/dares/glade-p-dares-Oct2023/Observations/land' 
setenv ocn_obspath    ''
setenv ice_obspath    ''
setenv obspaths       ($atm_obspath $lnd_obspath)
                       
# - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - 
# cesmroot        Location of the CESM code base.  This version of the script
#                 only supports version cesm2_1 or later, which is available from
#                 https://github.com/ESCOMP/cesm.
# use_SourceMods  DART mostly does not require a CESM SourceMods directory.
#                 One exception is to use SIF obs in CLM.
# SourceModDir    If you have modifications to CESM, they should be managed in your CESM git clone.
# cesmdata     Location of some supporting CESM data files such as
#              CAM external forcing files, which are specified in the setup script.  
#              It's not common to change these (or easy to figure out which are 
#              the most suitable; odd time series, averages, etc.)
#              They are found in $cesmdata and can be added to the user_nl_cam_####.
# caseroot     The CESM2 $CASEROOT directory, where the CESM+DART configuration files
#              will be stored and the build commands will be executed.
#              This is not defined here because it's different for CESM and each assimilator.
#              This should probably not be on a fileystem that is scrubbed.
#              This script WILL DELETE any existing caseroot, so this script,
#              and other useful things should be kept elsewhere.
# cime_output   $cime_output/$CASENAME is the directory where CESM's build and run directories will be created.
#               Large amount of space needed, generally on a scratch partition.  
#               CESM will define the following variables:
#               RUNDIR:      The CESM run directory.  Needs large amounts of disk.
#                            Default; $CIME_OUTPUT_ROOT/$CASE/run
#               EXEROOT:     The CESM executable directory.
#                            Default; $CIME_OUTPUT_ROOT/$CASE/bld
#               DOUT_S_ROOT: The CESM short-term archive directory.
#                            LARGE amount of disk.
#                            Files remain here until you move them or they are purged.
#                            Default; $CIME_OUTPUT_ROOT/archive/$CASE.
#                            DART's preference is to define the archive directory to be;
#                                     $CIME_OUTPUT_ROOT/$CASE/archive
#                            This change of DOUT_S_ROOT may interfere with the ability to clone your case.
# CIME_MODEL   Specifies the version of CESM to build.
#              This will override the value that may be set in your ~/.cime/config.

setenv cesmroot       /glade/work/${USER}/CESM/${cesmtag}
setenv use_SourceMods TRUE
setenv SourceModDir   ${dartroot}/models/clm/DART_SourceMods/cesm2_2_0/SourceMods
setenv cesmdata       /glade/campaign/cesm/cesmdata/cseg/inputdata
setenv exproot        /glade/work/raeder/Exp/${EXP}
# conventional; setenv caseroot       /glade/work/${USER}/cases/${cesmtag}/${EXP}
setenv cime_output    /glade/derecho/scratch/${USER}/${EXP}

# This will override the value that may be set in your ~/.cime/config.
setenv CIME_MODEL cesm
setenv CIMEROOT   $cesmroot/cime

# ==============================================================================
# Dates 
#
# If the ICs for the various components come from different CASEs,
# then the files must be renamed or linked to file names which use refcase
# and the ref date parameters.

# refcase    The name of the existing reference case from which this run will start.
#            The default script setup expects the initial condition files to use this
#            as the CASENAME part of the file names, and as the scratch directory name
#            where they are stored.

# NOTE:      All the ref* variables must be treated like strings and have
#            the appropriate number of preceeding zeros. 
# refyear    The specific date/time-of-day in the reference case that this
# refmon     run will start from.  (Also see 'runtime settings' below for
# refday     start_year, start_mon, start_day and start_tod.)
# reftod
#
# stagedir        The directory location of the reference case files.
# stage_inflation Whether the reference case files include inflation restart files
#                 "all"; expect to find files for all assimilating components 
#                 "some"; use what exists and let fill_inflation_restart provide the rest
#                 "none"; don't try to stage existing files.
#                 TODO; not implemented in stage_cesm_files yet.
# base_member     If the initial ensemble will be created by filter by perturbing around a truth,
#                 pick a member of the reference case ensemble.  
#                 It can be beyond the ensemble size of this assimilation.
#
# ==============================================================================

setenv refcase      clm5.0.06_f09_80
setenv refyear      2011
setenv refmon       01
setenv refday       01
setenv reftod       00000
# useful combinations of time that we use below
setenv refdate      ${refyear}-${refmon}-${refday}
setenv reftimestamp ${refyear}-${refmon}-${refday}-${reftod}

# Alternative reference case for different dates, cases, etc. may be kept in places like:
# setenv stagedir /glade/scratch/${USER}/${refcase}/run
setenv stagedir        /glade/derecho/scratch/${USER}/archive/${refcase}/rest/${reftimestamp}
# setenv stagedir /glade/campaign/cisl/dares/glade-p-dares-Oct2023/RDA_strawman/CESM_ensembles/CLM/CLM5BGC-Crop/ctsm_${reftimestamp}
setenv stage_inflation all

setenv base_member 7

# In a hybrid configuration, you can set the start_date to whatever you want.
# It does not have to match the reference (although changing the month is risky).
#
# start_year           Generally this is the same as the reference case date, 
# start_month          but it can be different if you want to start this run 
# start_day            as if it was a different time.
# start_tod
# start_date           The date used as the starting date for the hybrid run.

# stop_option          Units for determining the forecast length between assimilations
# stop_n               Number of time units in each forecast
# resubmit             How many times to resubmit a job if it fails.
#                      Should almost always be 0, because a failed job requires cleanup
#                      before continuing.
# dtime                Nominal timestep of the model (seconds). 1800 is the default.
#                      Component coupling times can be changed in the setup script,
#                      but must be consistent with this value.

setenv start_year    2011
setenv start_month   01
setenv start_day     01
setenv start_tod     00000
setenv start_date     ${start_year}-${start_month}-${start_day}

setenv stop_option  nhours
setenv stop_n       6
setenv resubmit     0

setenv dtime         1800

# ==============================================================================
# Archiving

# short_term_archiver  Copies the files from each job step to an archive directory.
# st_archive_queue     The short term archiver is a single process job,
#                      so it should not be submitted to a queue which assigns whole nodes.
# st_archive_time      Enough time for st_archive to finish.  Format HH:MM:SS
#                      (Omitting a part lets st_archive interpret it in suboptimal ways.
# save_restart_interval  Define how often sets of restart files will be archived,
#                        in order to conserve disk space during a multi-cycle job.
#                        Day-of-week or a number of days.
# save_stages_freq     The frequency at which to save the stages' members;
#                      NONE, RESTART_TIMES, ALL  (mean and sd always saved).
# archdir              The CESM short-term archive directory.
#                      Files will remain here until you move them to permanent storage or are purged.
#                      Requires large amounts of disk space. 

# ==============================================================================

setenv short_term_archiver   off
setenv st_archive_queue      develop
setenv st_archive_time       06:00:00
setenv save_restart_interval 3
setenv archdir               ${cime_output}/archive
#                            This translates into scratch.../$EXP/archive

# ==============================================================================
# OSSE/Perfect Model experiments only.
# If there is an ensemble of CLM states to choose from, which one do you want as
# the truth? There is an argument for picking an instance that will not be part
# of the ensemble used for the assimilation experiment. 

# setenv SingleInstanceRefcase FALSE
# setenv TRUTHinstance 80

# ==============================================================================
# Data models

# The default CAM sea surface temperature file is climatological,
# which is less than ideal for atmospheric assimilations.
# The supported alternative is time interpolation of a monthly SST+CICE data set.
# Examples are provided here.
# "2 degree":
# setenv sst_dataset ${cesmdata}/atm/cam/sst/sst_HadOIBl_bc_1.9x2.5_L30_1850_2022_c241003.nc
# setenv sst_grid ${cesmdata}/share/domains/domain.ocn.fv1.9x2.5_gx1v7.170518.nc
# "1 degree":
# setenv sst_dataset ${cesmdata}/atm/cam/sst/sst_HadOIBl_bc_0.9x1.25_1850_2016_c170525.nc
# setenv sst_grid ${cesmdata}/share/domains/domain.ocn.fv0.9x1.25_gx1v7.151020.nc

# "1/4 degree":
# A better alternative is daily, 1/4-degree SSTs from Reynolds,...,Tomas
# The file with the whole dataset is slow to read, so it has been divided into yearly files:
# /glade/work/raeder/Models/CAM_init/SST_ssh/Yearly/avhrr_v2.1.YYYY_12Z_filled_c250709.nc
# WARNING: if your assimilation sequence crosses a file timeseries limit you MUST change the 
#          > filename in SSTICE_DATA_FILENAME
#          > year in SSTICE_YEAR_{ALIGN,START,END}
#          in the env_run.xml files.  
#          CESM will likely make poor date choices if it can't find bracketing dates.

# Namelist variable taxmode (./setup_assimilation) determines how it does that calculation.
#   'cycle' (default) means calculate a stream time slot to use with a given model time.
#           A year different from the model year might be chosen.
#   'limit' means 'if stream data outside of the range _START to _END, then fail'
#           Assimilations should use this value.

# Specify the beginning and ending years of the data set.
# ./DART_config will set SSTICE_YEAR_START and _ALIGN to the same value, 
# which lines up the stream calendar with the model calendar.  
# CESM will then calculate which stream time slot is needed for a given model time.
# setenv sst_year_start 1850
# setenv sst_year_end   2022

setenv sst_dataset \
   "/glade/work/raeder/Models/CAM_init/SST_ssh/Yearly/avhrr_v2.1.2018_12Z_filled_c250709.nc"
   # "/glade/work/raeder/Models/CAM_init/SST_ssh/avhrr-only-v2.2011-202001_0Z_filled_c200810.nc"
   # "/glade/work/raeder/Models/CAM_init/SST/avhrr-only-v2.20100101_cat_20101231_filled_c130829.nc"
   # "/glade/work/raeder/Models/CAM_init/SST/avhrr-only-v2.20130101_cat_20130731_filled_c170223.nc"
setenv sst_year_start 2018
setenv sst_year_end   2018

set list = `ncdump -h $sst_dataset | grep calendar`
if ($list[3] !~ '"gregorian"') then
   echo "ERROR: $sst_dataset"
   echo "       must have the calendar attribute attached to the time variable."
   echo "       Use: ncatted -a calendar,time,c,c,gregorian $sst_dataset"
   exit 5
endif
set user_grid = "${user_grid} --gridfile /glade/work/raeder/Models/CAM_init/SST_ssh"
set user_grid = "${user_grid}/config_grids2.1_oi0.25.xml"

setenv sst_grid    /glade/work/raeder/Models/CAM_init/SST_ssh/domain.ocn.d025.120821.nc

# ==============================================================================
# machine-specific commands:
# job settings:
#
# PROJECT    CESM2 preferred name for account used to charge core hours.
#            Using setenv makes it available to utils/python/CIME/case.py/get_project
# email        raeder@ucar.edu
# machine    The name of the computer.  Used in create_newcase and some path names.
# queue      On derecho the 'queue' passed to create_newcase can be "main" or "preempt".
#            (if the preempt wrapper bash script assim_preempt.sh is available).
# priority   If queue = main, the desired priority (economy, regular, premium)
#            is passed to JOB_PRIORITY (env_run.xml)
# timewall   can be changed during a series by changing the case.run
#            Each batch system can have a different format (defined in config_batch.xml),
#            so learn whether you're specifying %H:%M:%S or %D:%H:%M 
#            and how short versions are interpreted.
# nodes_per_instance  The (usually minimum) number of nodes required to run a single instance.
#                     If you get 'out of memory' errors OR failures without any messages,
#                     try increasing the nodes_per_instance.
# use_tasks_per_node  The tasks/node to use.  Usually the number of physical processors.
# number_of_threads   threads/task to use,
#                     To be used by --pecount arg to create_namelist.
#                     In cases where there are few observations but a large memory requirement,
#                     it may be more efficient for DART to use fewer MPI tasks/node 
#                     than the number of available processors/node.
#                     But that would restrict the model to using fewer  MPI tasks per node.
#                     That can be compensated by telling it to use >1 (OpenMP) threads per node.
#                     E.g. change from 36 tasks x 1 thread  to  12 tasks x 3 threads.

setenv PROJECT      P86850054
setenv email        raeder@ucar.edu
setenv machine      derecho
setenv queue        main
# setenv queue        preempt
setenv priority     economy
setenv timewall     0:20:00
setenv nodes_per_instance  2
setenv use_tasks_per_node  128
setenv number_of_threads   1


# ==============================================================================
# standard commands:
#
# Make sure that this script is using standard system commands
# instead of aliases defined by the user.
# If the standard commands are not in the location listed below,
# change the 'set' commands to use them.
# The FORCE  options are not optional. You may need to specify full paths
# to alternate locations that support the '-f' option.
# The VERBOSE options are useful for debugging, but are optional because
# some systems don't like the -v option to any of the following.

# ==============================================================================
set nonomatch       # suppress "rm" warnings if wildcard does not match anything

set VERBOSE = '-v'
set    MOVE = "/usr/bin/mv $VERBOSE"
set    COPY = "/usr/bin/cp $VERBOSE --preserve=timestamps"
set    LINK = "/usr/bin/ln $VERBOSE -s"
set    LIST = "/usr/bin/ls "
set  REMOVE = "/usr/bin/rm -r"

